WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.389 --> 00:00:02.800
- [Narrator] If you go to
Mexico City, in the neighborhood

00:00:02.800 --> 00:00:05.390
Merced La Buena, in the
intersection of San Nicolas

00:00:05.390 --> 00:00:07.300
and Oriente, through this rusty iron door,

00:00:07.300 --> 00:00:09.850
and up two flights of stairs,
you will meet the family

00:00:09.850 --> 00:00:13.368
that changed Mexican folk art forever.

00:00:13.368 --> 00:00:15.400
(crickets chirping)

00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:18.520
Uh (clears throat), sorry, I
didn't mean to disturb you.

00:00:18.520 --> 00:00:22.270
(triumphant orchestra music)

00:00:24.909 --> 00:00:28.242
- (in foreign language)

00:00:29.719 --> 00:00:32.270
- [Narrator] Alebrijes,
the fantastical creatures

00:00:32.270 --> 00:00:34.970
traditionally made out of papier-mâché.

00:00:34.970 --> 00:00:36.550
Across Mexico, you can find

00:00:36.550 --> 00:00:39.180
these beautiful monsters everywhere.

00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:42.360
You can find them in the
streets, in the museums,

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:44.560
there are workshops of alebrijes scattered

00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:45.729
around the city.

00:00:45.729 --> 00:00:49.010
And there's even a huge
parade of monumental alebrijes

00:00:49.010 --> 00:00:51.083
that takes over Mexico City once a year.

00:00:52.090 --> 00:00:53.993
Ah, and you can catch them in Coco.

00:00:55.480 --> 00:00:57.106
- Are those...

00:00:57.106 --> 00:00:58.380
Alebrijes!

00:00:58.380 --> 00:01:00.827
- [Narrator] But what
exactly is an alebrijes?

00:01:05.889 --> 00:01:10.139
- [Leonardo] (in foreign language)

00:01:14.263 --> 00:01:15.105
(fire crackles)

00:01:18.580 --> 00:01:20.096
(in foreign language)

00:01:41.030 --> 00:01:42.550
- [Narrator] And all
of this vast tradition

00:01:42.550 --> 00:01:45.771
can be traced back to
one man, Pedro Linares,

00:01:45.771 --> 00:01:47.750
Leonardo's grandpa, who said that after

00:01:47.750 --> 00:01:50.600
falling into a coma, had a
nightmare where some very dark

00:01:50.600 --> 00:01:52.280
and horrific creatures kept screaming

00:01:52.280 --> 00:01:54.302
the word, "alebrije" to him.

00:01:54.302 --> 00:01:59.302
- [Creatures] "Alebrijes,
alebrijes" (whispers sinisterly).

00:02:00.982 --> 00:02:03.830
(sinister laugh)

00:02:03.830 --> 00:02:05.210
- [Narrator] Not long after waking up,

00:02:05.210 --> 00:02:07.531
he started building the first alebrijes.

00:02:07.531 --> 00:02:11.200
(light flute music)

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:15.417
And now his son and grandchildren
continue the tradition.

00:02:15.417 --> 00:02:18.750
- (in foreign language)

00:03:08.439 --> 00:03:10.856
(soft music)

